    <p class="ai-model-kicker">Frontier models</p>
    <h1 class="ai-model-title">Gemini 4 Carbon</h1>
    <p class="ai-model-dek">Gemini 4 Carbon is a Google DeepMind model announced 28 October 2026. Google calls it its strongest model, aimed at cybersecurity and long, multi-step work. It is not generally available. Google says it is giving Carbon first to a subset of cyber defenders, then to more trusted partners through the <a href="https://deepmind.google/fairwind-program/">Fairwind Program</a>, and will bring it to developers, businesses, and consumers later. Google says Carbon builds on <a href="/ai/models/google/gemini-4-argon/">Gemini 4 Argon</a>. It does not say Carbon replaces Argon. Google has not published an API id, the name a program uses to call the model. The introductory price is $2 per million input tokens and $10 per million output tokens, the same as Argon’s. Tokens are the word pieces models are billed by. Cached input, text the service has already seen and stored, is 95% off. After the intro period, $4 and $20 apply. Google gives no end date for Carbon’s intro price.</p>
    <hr class="ai-model-rule" />
    <ModelFacts />
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Training</h2>
    <p>Google says it trained Carbon to handle complex or high-pressure tasks the right way, and cut shortcut attempts during training. Google says Carbon brought successful cheating on Terminal-Bench 4.0 to 0.0%, with cheating attempts at 0.4%, while its overall score went up. Those are Google’s figures. Parameter count, training compute, dataset, and knowledge cutoff are not in the 28 October post.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Inference</h2>
    <p>Google says Carbon is built for long agent work, and that its own teams have seen it run for days on multi-step engineering jobs, such as finding and patching subtle bugs across large codebases. Google does not publish tokens per second, rate limits, regions, or thinking-level controls in the announce. Artificial Analysis tested Gemini 4 Carbon with high reasoning. Artificial Analysis lists text, image, video, and speech input, with text output. That list is Artificial Analysis’s, not a Google sentence.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Context</h2>
    <p>Context is how much text the model can take in and write in one job. Artificial Analysis lists a 1 million token context window for Gemini 4 Carbon. The 28 October Google post does not give a context length.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Methods</h2>
    <p>Google says Carbon uses the same layered safety setup as Argon: model training, real-time misuse defenses, prompt-injection resistance, and monitoring of the chain of thought. Google says it is taking part in the U.S. government’s voluntary process for testing models before release. In live coding with its own teams, Google says high-severity misalignment alerts fell by almost 80%, and in internal tests Carbon made about half as many unsupported claims as Argon. Google says Carbon had 0.0% attack success on Gray Swan’s Indirect Prompt Injection benchmark, against 0.7% for Argon. Indirect prompt injection means hidden instructions in a webpage, email, or file that try to hijack the model. Those are Google’s figures.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Benchmarking</h2>
    <p>Artificial Analysis scores Gemini 4 Carbon (high) at 59 on its Intelligence Index v5.0, fetched 28 October 2026. That is #1 on the v5.0 leaderboard, one point above Claude Opus 5.5 (max) at 58. Gemini 4 Argon (high) scores 50 on the same index. Artificial Analysis moved to Intelligence Index v5.0 on 27 October 2026, a new nine-test set on a new scale, so v5.0 scores do not line up with older v4.3.2 numbers. That is an independent score, not a Google chart number.</p>
    <p>Artificial Analysis says Carbon has the top score on 4 of the 9 Index tests, and that it is not leading in coding and knowledge work. On the Artificial Analysis Coding Agent Index, Carbon (high) in Antigravity CLI scores 59, behind Claude Code with Claude Opus 5.5 (max) at 62.</p>
    <p>On Terminal-Bench 4.0, <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/articles/gemini-4-carbon-new-number-1">Artificial Analysis</a> scores Gemini 4 Carbon (high) second at <strong>64%</strong>. On FrontierCode v1.1, Carbon scores <strong>51%</strong>, behind Opus 5.5 (max) at 56%. On AA-Omniscience v1.1, Carbon scores <strong>54</strong>, six points ahead of Claude Opus 5.5 (max) at 48 and eight points ahead of Gemini 4 Argon at 46. Those are independent scores, not Google chart numbers.</p>
    <p>The cells below are Google’s 28 October post. They are lab claims, and Google says they were run without guardrails.</p>
